(7517087, 1200354, 124729, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by SaraR:</b></div><hr/><i> Back to clarify my comment, especially in light of Ubique''s.  I love every aspect of this, except at the very bottom of the frame where the tree appears to grow out of the giraffe.  I really like the inclusion of the rest of it (and I concede it would look pretty silly without the bottom..) and love the way the giraffe''s head appears above the leaves.  I hadn''t consciously considered that the foreground tree/bush, acted as an echo of the form of the animal, but I appreciate the photo all the more for having that connection pointed out.  Edit: Deserved a much higher finish tan this </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Don''t worry Sara :)<br/>This is probably my favourite picture of my trip in South Africa, and make me smile every time I watch at it. Of course it would have been great to be in the Top 10, but I''m really happy to have it in my gallery.<br/> <br/>And as too often happens:<br/> <br/>Avg (participants): 5.6207<br/>Avg (non-participants): 6.7667<br/> <br/>This also make me smile :)<br/> <br/>Thank you all</td>', 'Originally posted by SaraR: Back to clarify my comment, especially in light of Ubique''s.  I love every aspect of this, except at the very bottom of the frame where the tree appears to grow out of the giraffe.  I really like the inclusion of the rest of it (and I concede it would look pretty silly without the bottom..) and love the way the giraffe''s head appears above the leaves.  I hadn''t consciously considered that the foreground tree/bush, acted as an echo of the form of the animal, but I appreciate the photo all the more for having that connection pointed out.  Edit: Deserved a much higher finish tan this  Don''t worry Sara :)This is probably my favourite picture of my trip in South Africa, and make me smile every time I watch at it. Of course it would have been great to be in the Top 10, but I''m really happy to have it in my gallery. And as too often happens: Avg (participants): 5.6207Avg (non-participants): 6.7667 This also make me smile :) Thank you all', '2017-06-05 05:17:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(7517118, 1200338, 189204, '<td valign="top">Thank you all for your comments. I recognized that the goose is out of focus and that its not dominating image enough. The "plan" was to have the fierce goose cover half or more of the image and that the DOF would be much shallower. When planning this shot i didn''t take into account the fear factor and the fact that had to get so close with the selected lens. I had couple better images in that session but since this was closest to the idea i had, and i took the nasty tumble when taking this, i thought that it would be fitting to submit this one. <br/>How could i have captured the focus better or is this just too ambitious of a plan? Or should the depth just be wide? Obviously next time i''ll rather take the hit from the goose than the rocks. Or wear a helmet :)</td>', 'Thank you all for your comments. I recognized that the goose is out of focus and that its not dominating image enough. The "plan" was to have the fierce goose cover half or more of the image and that the DOF would be much shallower. When planning this shot i didn''t take into account the fear factor and the fact that had to get so close with the selected lens. I had couple better images in that session but since this was closest to the idea i had, and i took the nasty tumble when taking this, i thought that it would be fitting to submit this one. How could i have captured the focus better or is this just too ambitious of a plan? Or should the depth just be wide? Obviously next time i''ll rather take the hit from the goose than the rocks. Or wear a helmet :)', '2017-06-05 08:52:47.000000', NULL, 0),
